@node XMR
@chapter XMR
@cindex XMR
@cindex Monero
@cindex mining calculator
@cindex P2Pool
@cindex monerod

The @dfn{XMR} screen gathers what there is to know about Monero on one
page.  It has seven framed panels in three bands:

@table @strong
@item Market
The price in US dollars with its change over 24 hours, the price in
roubles, euros and bitcoin, the 24-hour range, the change over 7, 30
and 60 days and a year (green when up, red when down), the market
capitalisation and rank, the 24-hour trading volume, the circulating
supply, the tail emission of 0.6 XMR a block as a yearly inflation
rate, the all-time high with its date, and how far the price is below
it.  The data comes from CoinGecko; @samp{priced} is the age of
CoinGecko's own quote.

@item XMR/USD
A Braille line chart of the last 30 days in orange, from four-hourly
Kraken candles (CoinGecko's hourly prices if Kraken fails), with a price
axis, a tick under every Monday, and the change, high, low, average and
daily volatility over the period.

@item Network
The block height, difficulty and network hash rate (difficulty divided
by the 120-second block target), the average time between the last 12
blocks (yellow above three minutes), the age of the newest block (yellow
after ten minutes), average fees per block and per transaction, the
current block reward, the transaction pool, the total number of
transactions, the hard-fork version and the median block size.  It comes
from the block explorer at @uref{https://xmrchain.net}.

@item Recent blocks
The last 12 blocks, as many as fit: height, age, number of
transactions (the coinbase not counted), size and the fees they paid.

@item P2Pool
One column per P2Pool sidechain (main, mini and nano by default): the
pool's hash rate (sidechain difficulty divided by its block time) and
its share of the network, the miners in the current PPLNS window and
all miners ever seen, the share difficulty, when and at which height
the pool last found a Monero block, the current and average effort
(yellow above 300%), the number of blocks found and the P2Pool version.
The data comes from @uref{https://p2pool.observer}.

@item Mining
What a miner of @code{hashrate_hs} would earn: its share of the network
hash rate, the expected XMR per day and per month (also in dollars and
in @code{currency}), the energy a @code{watts} rig uses per day and what
it costs at @code{price_per_kwh}, the profit per day after electricity
(red when negative), the XMR price at which mining breaks even, how
often it would find a block alone on average, and how many P2Pool
shares it would find per day on each sidechain (yellow below one a day,
since then payouts are rare).  The earnings are expected values:
@var{share} * 720 * @var{reward}, where 720 is the number of blocks
in a day and the reward is the latest block's (0.6 XMR of tail emission
plus fees).

@item Node
Your own @command{monerod}, if @code{node} is set: whether it is
synchronized, still starting (it has no peers yet, so it does not know
the chain's height), or how far it has got (a gauge, the blocks left and, from
the speed between two polls, an estimate of the time to finish), the
height compared to the explorer's, difficulty, transaction pool, the
size of the database and the free disk space (red below 20 GiB),
incoming and outgoing peer connections, RPC clients, the peer lists,
the daemon version, its uptime and whether its RPC is restricted.  A
restricted RPC (@option{--restricted-rpc}, the usual setting for a node
reachable by others) hides the version, uptime, free space and peers;
those show as @samp{hidden (restricted)}.  Without @code{node} the
panel explains how to set one up.
@end table

Each panel title says how old its data is.  When a refresh fails, the
title turns yellow and says when, the panel keeps showing the last data
and its bottom line gives the error; a panel that never had data shows
the error in red instead.  The groups are fetched independently, so one
service being down blanks nothing else.

On a 120-column terminal the blocks table drops its size and fee
columns when they do not fit, and every panel shows as many rows as
there is room for.

@section Running a node for the panel

@command{monerod} answers the panel's @samp{get_info} call on its RPC
port, 18081 by default.  To let the Pi reach a node on another machine,
bind the RPC to the LAN:

@example
monerod --rpc-bind-ip 0.0.0.0 --confirm-external-bind --restricted-rpc
@end example

@noindent
and set @code{node = "http://@var{address}:18081"} in the @code{[xmr]}
table.  Without @option{--restricted-rpc} the panel shows more (version,
uptime, free space, peers), but then anyone on the network can call
administrative methods, so prefer it only on a trusted network, or run
raspdash on the node's own machine with @samp{http://127.0.0.1:18081}.
RPC logins (@option{--rpc-login}) are not supported.

@section Configuration

@example
[xmr]
hashrate_hs = 10000
watts = 65
price_per_kwh = 6.5
currency = "rub"
# node = "http://192.168.1.10:18081"
node_direct = true
pools = ["main", "mini", "nano"]
timezone = "Europe/Moscow"
# explorer = "https://xmrchain.net"
market_interval = 600
chart_interval = 1800
network_interval = 120
pools_interval = 300
node_interval = 30
@end example

@table @code
@item hashrate_hs
The hash rate of the rig in the mining calculator, in hashes per
second.  Default 10000.
@item watts
Its power draw in watts.  Default 65.
@item price_per_kwh
The price of a kilowatt-hour, in @code{currency}.  Default 6.5.
@item currency
The currency of @code{price_per_kwh}, as CoinGecko names it
(@samp{rub}, @samp{usd}, @samp{eur}, @dots{}).  Earnings are converted
into it with CoinGecko's XMR price; profit and break-even are shown
only when CoinGecko knows the currency.  Default @samp{rub}.
@item node
The RPC address of your @command{monerod}, such as
@samp{http://192.168.1.10:18081}.  @samp{/json_rpc} is added, and
@samp{http://} when no scheme is given.  Default: none.
@item node_direct
Talk to the node directly, bypassing any proxy in @env{http_proxy}
and friends; a proxy on the way to a LAN address usually fails.  Set it
to @code{false} for a remote node that must go through the proxy.
Default @code{true}.
@item pools
The P2Pool sidechains to show, in order: any of @samp{main},
@samp{mini} and @samp{nano}.  Default all three.
@item timezone
The zone whose Mondays mark the chart's time axis.  Default
@samp{Europe/Moscow}.
@item explorer
The base address of an explorer running onion-monero-blockchain-explorer
with its JSON API enabled.  Default @samp{https://xmrchain.net}.
@item market_interval
Seconds between CoinGecko market fetches.  Default 600.
@item chart_interval
Seconds between price-chart fetches.  Default 1800.
@item network_interval
Seconds between explorer fetches (network and recent blocks).  Default
120.
@item pools_interval
Seconds between P2Pool fetches.  Default 300.
@item node_interval
Seconds between @samp{get_info} calls to the node.  Default 30.
@end table

No interval can be set below 10 seconds.  The public services allow far
more requests than these defaults make: about 15 requests every 10
minutes in all, plus the node's.

@section Internals

@file{src/xmr.cpp} holds five sources, @samp{xmr-market},
@samp{xmr-chart}, @samp{xmr-network}, @samp{xmr-pools} and
@samp{xmr-node} (only with @code{node}), and the screen, which draws the
bands with @code{tui::Rect} and one function per panel.  The parsers
and the arithmetic (@code{parse_market}, @code{parse_network_info},
@code{parse_blocks}, @code{parse_pool}, @code{parse_node_info},
@code{sync_fraction}, @code{estimate}, @code{pool_shares_per_day} and
the formatters) are pure functions declared in @file{src/xmr.hpp} and
tested by @file{tests/test_xmr.cpp} against responses saved in
@file{tests/data/xmr-*.json}.

@table @asis
@item Market
@uref{https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/coins/monero}, with tickers,
community and developer data turned off; @code{market_data} holds the
prices per currency.  A rate-limit answer (@samp{status.error_message})
becomes the panel's error.
@item Chart
@uref{https://api.kraken.com/0/public/OHLC?pair=XMRUSD&interval=240},
or @samp{coins/monero/market_chart?vs_currency=usd&days=30} from
CoinGecko.  They are parsed by the Markets screen's parsers
(@pxref{Markets}) and cut to the last 30 days.
@item Network
@samp{/api/networkinfo} and @samp{/api/transactions?limit=12} (with a 60-second timeout, since
the answer is about 150 kB) of the
explorer.  @samp{difficulty} arrives as a string.  The transaction list
leaves out each block's coinbase, so the per-block reward is not in it;
the reward shown is P2Pool's @samp{mainchain.reward}, or 0.6 XMR plus
the newest block's fees when no pool answered.
@item P2Pool
@samp{/api/pool_info} on @samp{p2pool.observer}, @samp{mini.} and
@samp{nano.p2pool.observer}.  The site turns away clients whose
User-Agent looks like a script (curl's, for example) with a challenge
page; raspdash's own User-Agent passes, so keep @code{[app] user_agent}
a real product string (@pxref{Application settings}).
@item Node
A JSON-RPC @samp{get_info} POST to @samp{@var{node}/json_rpc}.  A
restricted RPC reports @samp{free_space} as the largest 64-bit number
and @samp{start_time} as 0; both are treated as unknown.  The source
keeps the previous answer to estimate the sync speed, smoothed over
polls.
@end table

Every response is saved in the cache directory (@file{xmr-market-coingecko.json},
@file{xmr-chart-chart.json}, @file{xmr-network-networkinfo.json},
@file{xmr-network-blocks.json}, @file{xmr-pools-pool-main.json},
@dots{}, @file{xmr-node-get_info.json}) and parsed again at start, so a
restart shows the last data at once.
